    "result_text": "The build is green. Let me verify the full change is coherent by reviewing the audit log line in the controller, which also uses the membership number \u2014 confirmed it printed `membershipNumber=NSW-16E6609E-M26` (FY26 for the 2026-10-02 registration), consistent with the new fiscal-year logic.\n\nSummary of the change \u2014 date-derived values now sit on a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starts 1 July):\n\n- **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`**\n  - Added a new read-only `fiscalYear` field (`FY<YY>`), documented as derived from the business-day-adjusted registration date.\n  - Updated `membershipNumber`'s description: the `<YY>` segment is now the fiscal year, not the registration calendar year.\n  - Updated `membershipPoints`'s description: the tenure bonus now triggers on an elapsed fiscal year rather than a 365-day window.\n\n- **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`**\n  - Added a `fiscalYear` mapping plus `formatFiscalYear(...)`, formatting `FY<YY>` from the registration date.\n  - Added a private `fiscalYearStart(LocalDate)` helper \u2014 returns the calendar year the fiscal year begins (month \u2265 July \u2192 same year, else prior year).\n  - `formatMembershipNumber(...)` now uses `fiscalYearStart(...) % 100` for the `M<YY>` segment.\n  - `formatMembershipPoints(...)` awards the +3 tenure bonus when `fiscalYearStart(now) - fiscalYearStart(registrationDate) >= 1` (registration in a prior fiscal year), replacing the 365-day comparison. Removed the now-unused `ChronoUnit` import.\n\nA note on the two interpretive choices I made (no test pins them, so I picked the reading that keeps the existing example/behaviour intact):\n- **`FY<YY>` names the year the fiscal year *starts*** \u2014 so a 2026-10-02 registration yields `FY26` and `M26`, preserving the spec's existing `NSW-1A2B3C4D-M26` example.\n- **Tenure bonus = \"at least one elapsed fiscal year\"** (`>= 1`), i.e. the registration fiscal year precedes the current one \u2014 the natural fiscal-year analog of the old \"more than a year\" rule. If you intended strictly more than one full fiscal year (`>= 2`), that's a one-character change.\n\nTests: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
